      {
        "id": "lem-cg-comparison-convexity-and-model-spaces",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Comparison triangles in the Euclidean plane and the round sphere, model spaces, and CAT(0) and CAT(1) consequences",
        "deps": [
          "def-cg-cat-zero-cat-one-and-local-geodesic",
          "def-metric-space",
          "def-metric-ball",
          "def-metric-continuity",
          "def-metric-compactness",
          "def-geodesic-and-geodesic-metric-space",
          "def-complete-metric-space",
          "def-cauchy-in-metric",
          "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
          "def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine",
          "def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series",
          "thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges",
          "thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas",
          "cor-pi-is-the-first-positive-sine-zero",
          "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "lem-metrics-on-rn",
          "thm-compact-implies-complete-and-totally-bounded",
          "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "def-isometry-and-metric-embedding",
          "lem-metric-reverse-triangle",
          "def-upper-bound"
        ],
        "statement": "**(i) Euclidean comparison.** For all reals $a,b,c\\ge0$ with $a\\le b+c$, $b\\le c+a$, $c\\le a+b$ there are $\\bar p,\\bar q,\\bar r\\in\\mathbb E^2$ with the three prescribed distances, and any two such triangles are related by an isometry of $\\mathbb E^2$ ([[def-cg-cat-zero-cat-one-and-local-geodesic]]). Consequently every geodesic triangle in every metric space has a comparison triangle in $\\mathbb E^2$, and the plane is CAT(0): for a triangle in $\\mathbb E^2$ the comparison triangle is congruent to it and the defining inequality is an equality.\n\n**(ii) The round sphere.** For $n\\ge2$ the function $d_S(x,y)=\\arccos(x\\cdot y)$ is a metric on $S^{n-1}\\subset\\mathbb R^n$; $(S^{n-1},d_S)$ is a geodesic space whose geodesic segments are the minimal great-circle arcs; two points at distance $<\\pi$ are joined by a unique geodesic segment; and every closed ball of radius $<\\pi/2$ is convex ([[def-metric-ball]]). For every triple of side lengths with perimeter $<2\\pi$ satisfying the triangle inequalities there is a comparison triangle in $S^2$, unique up to an isometry of $S^2$. Hence $S^2$ is CAT(1), the CAT(1) inequality for a triangle in $S^2$ of perimeter $<2\\pi$ being an equality.\n\n**(iii) Spherical cosine rule and midpoint identity.** For $A,B,C\\in S^2$ with $a=d_S(B,C)$, $b=d_S(C,A)$, $c=d_S(A,B)<\\pi$ and vertex angle $\\gamma$ at $C$, $\\cos c=\\cos a\\cos b+\\sin a\\sin b\\cos\\gamma$. If $c<\\pi$, $a$ is the midpoint of a geodesic $[y,z]$ of length $c$ and $x\\in S^2$, then $$\\cos d_S(x,a)=\\frac{\\cos d_S(x,y)+\\cos d_S(x,z)}{2\\cos(c/2)},\\qquad \\cos(c/2)>0.$$\n\n**(iv) Consequences of CAT(0).** Let $X$ be CAT(0). Then: (a) geodesic segments between two points are unique and vary continuously with their endpoints ([[def-geodesic-and-geodesic-metric-space]]); (b) if $\\gamma,\\delta$ are geodesics with a common initial point and proportional parametrizations, then $d(\\gamma(t),\\delta(t))\\le(1-t)d(\\gamma(0),\\delta(0))+t\\,d(\\gamma(1),\\delta(1))$ for $t\\in[0,1]$; (c) (hinged criterion) for a geodesic triangle with vertices $z,x,y$ and the point $p_t$ on $[x,y]$ at distance $t\\,d(x,y)$ from $x$, the CAT(0) inequality $d(z,p_t)\\le d_2(\\bar z,\\bar p_t)$ holds if and only if $$d(z,p_t)^2\\le(1-t)d(z,x)^2+t\\,d(z,y)^2-t(1-t)d(x,y)^2,$$ the right-hand side being the squared Euclidean comparison distance; consequently a geodesic space is CAT(0) if and only if for every geodesic triangle and every point $x$ on a side the comparison inequality $d(p,x)\\le d_2(\\bar p,\\bar x)$ with the opposite vertex $p$ holds; (d) for every pair $y,y'$, every midpoint $m$ of a geodesic segment $[y,y']$ and every $z$, $d(z,m)^2\\le\\tfrac12(d(z,y)^2+d(z,y')^2)-\\tfrac14 d(y,y')^2$.\n\n**(v) CAT(1) short-geodesic estimates.** Let $X$ be CAT(1), $p\\in X$, $0<r<\\pi/2$ and $x,y,z\\in B(p,r)$. Then $B(p,r)$ is convex, $[y,z]$ is unique with midpoint $a$, and $$\\cos d(x,a)\\ge\\frac{\\cos d(x,y)+\\cos d(x,z)}{2\\cos(d(y,z)/2)},$$ where $d(y,z)\\le2r<\\pi$ makes the denominator positive; the perimeter $d(x,y)+d(y,z)+d(z,x)<3r<2\\pi$ is admissible for the CAT(1) test used.\n\n**(vi) The round circle.** For every $\\ell>0$, $d_\\ell$ is a metric on $S^1_\\ell$ ([[def-cg-cat-zero-cat-one-and-local-geodesic]]); $(S^1_\\ell,d_\\ell)$ is a compact complete geodesic space locally isometric to $\\mathbb R$, contains an isometrically embedded circle of length $\\ell$, and is CAT(1) if and only if $\\ell\\ge2\\pi$. For $\\ell<2\\pi$ the points $0,\\ell/3,2\\ell/3$ form a triangle of perimeter $\\ell<2\\pi$ whose side midpoint is at distance $\\ell/2$ from the opposite vertex, which exceeds the distance in the spherical comparison triangle; for $\\ell\\ge2\\pi$ every triangle of perimeter $<2\\pi$ lies in an arc of length $<\\pi$, hence is degenerate and realizes its comparison triangle isometrically.",
        "strategy": "(i) Lay the two given segments at a vertex and apply the Euclidean law of cosines; uniqueness is the classical congruence of triangles with three side lengths. (ii) $d_S$ is well defined because $x\\cdot y\\in[-1,1]$ and $\\arccos$ is injective on $[0,\\pi]$ ([[def-principal-inverse-sine-and-cosine]]); symmetry and separation are immediate, and the triangle inequality follows from the spherical cosine rule proved in (iii) and the monotonicity of $\\cos$ on $[0,\\pi]$ ([[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]]): for distinct $A,B,C$ with $a=d(B,C)$, $b=d(C,A)$ and vertex angle $\\gamma\\in[0,\\pi]$ at $C$, $\\cos c=\\cos a\\cos b+\\sin a\\sin b\\cos\\gamma\\ge\\cos(a+b)$, with equality only when $\\gamma=\\pi$; hence $c\\le a+b$ with equality exactly on a great arc. The parametrized minimal great arcs $c(t)=(\\cos t)A+(\\sin t)u$ satisfy $d_S(c(t),c(t'))=|t-t'|$ and are geodesics; uniqueness for $d_S<\\pi$ uses the positive cone spanned by two independent unit vectors ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]]); convexity of balls of radius $<\\pi/2$ follows by writing points in the positive cone (Bridson\u2013Haefliger I.2.3). Comparison triangles in $S^2$ exist and are unique up to isometry by the law of cosines, which exhibits a unique angle $\\gamma\\in[0,\\pi]$ and a placement of two unit vectors meeting at it; the perimeter bound $<2\\pi$ is what makes the side $c<\\pi$ and the computation admissible (Bridson\u2013Haefliger I.2.14). For a triangle in $S^2$ of perimeter $<2\\pi$ the isometry class of the comparison triangle is that of the triangle itself, so the CAT(1) inequality holds with equality. (iii) Expand $(B|C)=\\cos a\\cos b+\\sin a\\sin b\\,(u|v)$ along the initial vectors, and add the two cosine rules for the triangles $x,y,a$ and $x,z,a$; the two angles at the midpoint $a$ are supplementary, so their cosines cancel and the displayed identity results. (iv) Uniqueness of geodesics is (BH II.1.4(1)): two geodesics from $p$ to $q$ give a degenerate comparison triangle whose comparison points coincide. Convexity of distance between proportional geodesics and the hinged criterion are the standard comparison computations in $\\mathbb E^2$: the Euclidean identity $|\\bar z-\\bar p_t|^2=(1-t)|\\bar z-\\bar x|^2+t|\\bar z-\\bar y|^2-t(1-t)|\\bar x-\\bar y|^2$ converts the CAT(0) inequality into the displayed squared inequality, and (BH II.1.7(2)) shows that testing pairs (vertex, point on the opposite side) is equivalent to the full CAT(0) inequality; the midpoint form (d) is the case $t=1/2$. (v) Apply the CAT(1) inequality to the triangle $(x,y,z)$, admissible because its perimeter is $<3r<2\\pi$, and evaluate the comparison point of the midpoint by (iii); convexity of $B(p,r)$ is (BH II.1.4(3)). (vi) $d_\\ell$ is well defined and a metric because the minimum is attained on the finite set of nearest translates and the quotient $\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R/\\ell\\mathbb Z$ is compatible with the metric; the shorter arc is the unique geodesic for distances $<\\ell/2$, the space is the continuous image of the compact interval $[0,\\ell]$, hence complete ([[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]], [[thm-compact-implies-complete-and-totally-bounded]]), and balls of radius $<\\ell/3$ are isometric to intervals. For the CAT(1) criterion: if all three cyclic gaps of a triple were $<\\ell/2$ its perimeter would be $\\ell\\ge2\\pi$; otherwise the three points lie in a complementary arc of length $s\\le\\ell/2$ and the perimeter is $2s$, so perimeter $<2\\pi$ forces $s<\\pi$, and the triangle is degenerate on an interval of length $<\\pi$, where the spherical comparison is an isometry. For $\\ell<2\\pi$ the equally spaced triple has side midpoint at distance $\\ell/2$ from the opposite vertex, while the spherical comparison point is at distance $\\arccos\\bigl(\\cos(\\ell/3)/\\cos(\\ell/6)\\bigr)$ by (iii); the addition formula gives $2\\cos(\\ell/2)\\cos(\\ell/6)=\\cos(2\\ell/3)+\\cos(\\ell/3)<2\\cos(\\ell/3)$, since $\\cos(2\\ell/3)<\\cos(\\ell/3)$ for $0<\\ell<2\\pi$ by strict monotonicity of $\\cos$ on $[0,\\pi]$ together with $\\cos A-\\cos B=-2\\sin\\tfrac{A+B}{2}\\sin\\tfrac{A-B}{2}$, so $\\cos(\\ell/2)<\\cos(\\ell/3)/\\cos(\\ell/6)$ and the CAT(1) inequality fails.",
