        "statement": "**(i) Alexandrov's Lemma.** Let $\\kappa\\in\\{0,1\\}$, let $\\mathbb M^2_\\kappa$ be $\\mathbb E^2$ for $\\kappa=0$ and $S^2$ for $\\kappa=1$, and let $A,B,B',C$ be four distinct points of $\\mathbb M^2_\\kappa$; if $\\kappa=1$ assume $d(C,B)+d(C,B')+d(A,B)+d(A,B')<2\\pi$. Suppose $B$ and $B'$ lie on opposite sides of the geodesic line through $A$ and $C$. Let $\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$ and $\\alpha',\\beta',\\gamma'$ be the angles of the triangles $\\Delta(A,B,C)$ and $\\Delta(A,B',C)$ at $A,B,C$ and $A,B',C$. If $\\gamma+\\gamma'\\ge\\pi$, then\n\n(1) $d(B,C)+d(B',C)\\le d(B,A)+d(B',A)$;\n\n(2) if $\\Delta''$ is a triangle with vertices $\\bar A,\\bar B,\\bar B'$ such that $d(\\bar A,\\bar B)=d(A,B)$, $d(\\bar A,\\bar B')=d(A,B')$ and $d(\\bar B,\\bar B')=d(B,C)+d(C,B')$, and $\\bar C\\in[\\bar B,\\bar B']$ satisfies $d(\\bar B,\\bar C)=d(B,C)$, then the angle $\\bar\\alpha$ of $\\Delta''$ at $\\bar A$, the angle $\\bar\\beta'$ of $\\Delta''$ at $\\bar B'$ and the distance $d(\\bar A,\\bar C)$ satisfy $\\bar\\alpha\\ge\\alpha+\\alpha'$, $\\bar\\beta'\\ge\\beta'$ and $d(\\bar A,\\bar C)\\ge d(A,C)$; equality in any one of the three assertions implies the others and holds if and only if $\\gamma+\\gamma'=\\pi$.\n\n**(ii) Gluing Lemma for triangles.** Let $X$ be a metric space in which every pair of points at distance $<D_\\kappa$ is joined by a geodesic, and let $\\Delta=([q_0,q_1],[q_1,p],[p,q_0])$ be a geodesic triangle in $X$ of perimeter $<2D_\\kappa$ with distinct vertices. Let $r\\in[q_0,q_1]$ with $r\\ne p$, let $[p,r]$ be a geodesic, and put $\\Delta_1=([q_0,r],[r,p],[p,q_0])$ and $\\Delta_2=([p,r],[r,q_1],[q_1,p])$. If for $i=1,2$ every vertex angle of a comparison triangle $\\bar\\Delta_i$ in $\\mathbb M^2_\\kappa$ is no smaller than the corresponding angle of $\\Delta_i$, then the same is true for the angles of any comparison triangle for $\\Delta$ ([[def-cg-cat-zero-cat-one-and-local-geodesic]]).\n\n**(iii) Patchwork.** Let $X$ be a metric space of curvature $\\le\\kappa$, let $p,q_0,q_1\\in X$ with $q_0\\ne q_1$, $p\\notin[q_0,q_1]$, and $d(p,q_0)+d(p,q_1)+d(q_0,q_1)<2D_\\kappa$, let $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to X$ be a geodesic from $q_0$ to $q_1$, and let $c_s:[0,1]\\to X$, $s\\in[0,1]$, be geodesics from $p$ to $\\gamma(s)$ depending continuously on $s$ with $c_0=[p,q_0]$ and $c_1=[p,q_1]$. Then the vertex angles of the triangle $\\Delta=([q_0,q_1],[q_1,p],[p,q_0])$ are no greater than the corresponding angles of any comparison triangle in $\\mathbb M^2_\\kappa$. The same conclusion holds for a finite subdivision obtained by successive applications of (ii), provided each intermediate union is a geodesic triangle of perimeter $<2D_\\kappa$ and its common-side splitting point lies on the opposite geodesic side.\n\n**(iv) Upper-angle facts.** For nonconstant geodesic segments with a common initial point, restricting either segment to any nonzero initial subsegment leaves their Alexandrov upper angle unchanged. The two opposite directions at an interior point of a geodesic have upper angle $\\pi$. For any three nonconstant geodesic segments $a,b,c$ with the same initial point, their upper angles satisfy $\\angle(a,c)\\le\\angle(a,b)+\\angle(b,c)$.",
        "strategy": "First prove the short spherical boundary loop lies in a common open hemisphere, then use gnomonic projection to obtain the convex orientation at A and C inside triangle ABB\u2032. This derives u+y<\u03c0 and excludes \u03b1+\u03b1\u2032>\u03c0 from the original source perimeter hypothesis. Straighten the hinge and apply strict cosine monotonicity to all comparisons and their simultaneous equality cases. Prove the upper-angle triangle inequality locally for the gluing step. For patchwork use the source 2n\u22121 local triangles per strip, checking the total perimeter at each merge before joining strips. Clause (iv) exposes the initial-restriction and opposite-direction facts derived directly from the upper-angle definition in F5, and the upper-angle triangle inequality proved locally in step 2.3.",
