        ],
        "statement": "**(i) Berestovskii's theorem.** Let $L$ be a metric space, let $d_\\pi:=\\min\\{\\pi,d\\}$, and let $C(L)=\\{o\\}\\cup((0,\\infty)\\times L)$ be the Euclidean cone with the apex $o$ separately included and the metric $d_C(o,(r,x))=r$, $d_C^2((r,x),(s,y))=r^2+s^2-2rs\\cos d_\\pi(x,y)$ ([[def-cg-euclidean-cone-and-spherical-join-metrics]]). Then $C(L)$ is CAT(0) if and only if $(L,d_\\pi)$ is CAT(1) ([[def-cg-cat-zero-cat-one-and-local-geodesic]], [[lem-cg-comparison-convexity-and-model-spaces]]). With the convention $C(\\varnothing)=\\{o\\}$, the empty link is CAT(1) vacuously and its cone is the one-point CAT(0) space, so the equivalence also covers $L=\\varnothing$.\n\n**(ii) Polyhedral link criterion.** Let $X$ be a connected isometric polyhedral gluing with finitely many shapes and local finiteness, with its chain metric ([[def-cg-abstract-isometric-polyhedral-gluing-and-chain-metric]], [[thm-cg-polyhedral-chain-metric-topology-and-properness]]), let $F$ be a $k$-dimensional face and let $p\\in\\operatorname{relint}F$. Then $X$ is locally CAT(0) at $p$ if and only if the angular link $\\operatorname{Lk}_X(F)$, with its truncated metric, is CAT(1). A sufficiently small ball about $p$ is isometric to the ball of the same radius about $(0,o)$ in $\\mathbb R^k\\times C(\\operatorname{Lk}_X(F))$ with the product metric ([[thm-cg-cone-join-metric-and-local-product-chart]]). In particular $X$ is locally CAT(0) if and only if the angular link of every vertex of $X$ is CAT(1).",
        "strategy": "Classify cone geodesics by equality in the planar cone triangle inequality. Forward CAT comparison uses variable chord radii on both sides and the CAT(0) all-pairs inequality. Reverse comparison proves the squared vertex-to-side inequality by explicit sine interpolation for both small and large angular perimeters, and by direct expansion for antipodal sides. Radial similarities turn CAT(0) near the product-cone origin into CAT(0) globally. Matching active-facet tangent gluings at a face point and a point on its ray in a vertex cone proves the vertex reduction.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
